  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM EDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nCurrently, a MCV is moving southeast across northwest Tennessee into \ntoward the southern Plateau by late this afternoon. Airmass is \nmarginally unstable across the southern Plateau and southeast \nTennessee. Expect scattered showers and thunder to move into this \nregion by late this afternoon through the early evening hours. Main \nconcern is windy west to northwest winds along outflow boundary with \ngusts up to 30-35 mph.\n\nOtherwise, satellite shows some CI development across the far east \nTennessee mountains. Isolated to scattered showers are possible \nthere through early evening.\n\nFor tonight, mainly dry with a good deal of high and some mid-level \nclouds early then clearing. Some fog development is expected \nespecially near area-waterways.\n\nFor Friday, a weak short-wave will move into the region with \nassociated frontal boundary. Instability and moisture increases over \nthe area with MLCAPES of 1500-2000. DCAPE of 700-900 noted with mid-\nlevel lapse rates of 6 to 6.5 degrees. Shear is almost non existent. \nEnough mid-level dry air to support strong and gusty winds with the \nstronger storms.\n\nFor Saturday, an inverted trough axis is over the eastern sections \nof Tennessee with convergence and upslope flow into the far east \nTennessee mountains and southwest North Carolina. Afternoon \nconvection is expected there, otherwise dry.\n\nFor Sunday into early next week, upper ridge moves east into the \nsouthern Appalachians. Rising heights and strong subsidence will \nonce again produce mainly dry with hot and muggy conditions. Highs \nin the upper 80s to mid 90s are anticipated with heat indices across \nmuch of the valley increasing to 100-105 degrees. Some isolated \nstorms are possible across the orographic features but the main \nstory will be the return of the heat.",
